T* (post permanent directional signs bearing crosses on) Tj
T* (municipal property. Lost animal signs and other private) Tj
T* (postings often remain undisturbed by Borough officials.) Tj
T* (Orange ribbons were affixed to utility poles "for a lengthy) Tj
T* (period of time" by supporters of the local high school during) Tj
T* (a protracted controversy over school regionalization, but) Tj
T* (Borough officials made no effort to remove them. Every) Tj
T* (year, officials in the small community permit the local) Tj
T* (Chamber of Commerce to affix holiday displays to the) Tj
T* (Borough's utility poles for approximately six weeks during) Tj
T* (the Christmas holiday season. Red ribbons, wreaths, and) Tj
T* (seasonal holiday lights are attached to the Borough's utility) Tj
T* (poles as part of these displays.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The plaintiffs in this case are Orthodox Jewish residents) Tj
T* (of Tenafly3 whose faith forbids them from pushing or) Tj
T* (carrying objects outside their homes on the Sabbath or) Tj
T* (Yom Kippur.4 In accordance with a religious convention) Tj
T* (practiced by Orthodox Jews for over two thousand years,) Tj
T* (however, the plaintiffs believe they may engage in such) Tj
T* (activities outside their homes on the Sabbath within an) Tj
T* (eruv, a ceremonial demarcation of an area. Tenafly Eruv) Tj
T* (Ass'n, Inc. v. Borough of Tenafly, 155 F. Supp. 2d 142, 146) Tj
T* (\(D.N.J. 2001\). An eruv extends the space within which) Tj
T* (pushing and carrying is permitted on the Sabbath beyond) Tj
T* (the boundaries of the home, thereby enabling, for example,) Tj
T* (the plaintiffs to push baby strollers and wheelchairs, and) Tj
T* (carry canes and walkers, when traveling between home and) Tj
T* (synagogue. Without an eruv Orthodox Jews who have small) Tj
T* (children or are disabled typically cannot attend synagogue) Tj
T* (on the Sabbath.) Tj
T* (_________________________________________________________________) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (3. Tenafly Eruv Association, Inc. \("TEAI"\) is an organization formed to) Tj
T* (promote the creation of an eruv in Tenafly. Chaim Book, Yosifa Book,) Tj
T* (and Stefanie Dardik Gotlieb live in Tenafly. At the time of briefing in this) Tj
T* (appeal, Stephen Brenner was not a Tenafly resident, but was building a) Tj
T* (house in Tenafly and planned to move there once it was completed. For) Tj
T* (simplicity, we refer to TEAI and the individual plaintiffs collectively as) Tj
T* ("the plaintiffs" throughout this opinion.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (4. For simplicity, we refer throughout the remainder of our opinion only) Tj
T* (to the Sabbath.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                5) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Centuries ago, an eruv would be built using ropes and) Tj
T* (wooden poles. Today, Orthodox Jews can construct an eruv) Tj
T* (by attaching lechis--thin black strips made of the same) Tj
T* (hard plastic material as, and nearly identical to, the) Tj
T* (coverings on ordinary ground wires--vertically along utility) Tj
T* (poles. Along with preexisting horizontal overhead utility) Tj
T* (lines, the lechis designate an eruv's boundaries.5 Unless one) Tj
T* (knows which black plastic strips are lechis and which are) Tj
T* (utility wires, it is "absolutely impossible" to distinguish the) Tj
T* (two. Id. at 149. Throughout this litigation, the plaintiffs) Tj
T* (have maintained that an eruv \(as well as each constituent) Tj
T* (lechi\) is "not a religious symbol," but rather is an item with) Tj
T* ("religious significance." Id. at 148.) Tj
