T* () Tj
T* (On June 1, 1999, Erez Gotlieb and Gary Osen, two) Tj
T* (Orthodox Jews who are not parties to this case, met with) Tj
T* (Tenafly Mayor Ann Moscovitz to discuss creating an eruv in) Tj
T* (the Borough. Gotlieb and Osen met with Moscovitz because) Tj
T* (under Orthodox Jewish law an eruv is not valid unless a) Tj
T* (civil official with jurisdiction over the circumscribed area) Tj
T* (issues a ceremonial proclamation "renting" the area for a) Tj
T* (nominal fee \(e.g., one dollar\). The Mayor said she lacked) Tj
T* (authority to issue the requested proclamation, but agreed) Tj
T* (to bring the matter to the attention of the Borough Council,) Tj
T* (the Borough's legislative branch.6 She did not mention) Tj
T* (Ordinance 691 or suggest that affixing lechis  to utility poles) Tj
T* (might violate any other ordinance.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (At the next Council meeting, on July 8, 1999, the) Tj
T* (Council and approximately thirty Tenafly residents debated) Tj
T* (whether the Borough should grant the proclamation. Many) Tj
T* (of those present expressed vehement objections prompted) Tj
T* (by their fear that an eruv would encourage Orthodox Jews) Tj
T* (to move to Tenafly. A Council member whom the District) Tj
T* (Court was unable to identify noted "a concern that the) Tj
T* (_________________________________________________________________) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (5. Many major cities across the United States--such as Washington,) Tj
T* (D.C., New York, Chicago, Philadelphia, Los Angeles, Baltimore, Atlanta,) Tj
T* (and Cincinnati--have one or more eruvs. Both the White House and the) Tj
T* (United States Supreme Court are within the boundaries of an eruv.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (6. Six Council members compose the Borough's legislative branch. The) Tj
T* (Mayor does not participate in lawmaking unless the Council is) Tj
T* (deadlocked, in which case she casts the tie-breaking vote.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                6) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Orthodoxy would take over" Tenafly. Id. at 151-52. One) Tj
T* (Council member voiced his "serious concern" that "Ultra-) Tj
T* (Orthodox" Jews might "stone[ ] cars that drive down the) Tj
T* (streets on the Sabbath." Id. at 153-54. The Borough) Tj
T* (Attorney participated in the debate. Neither he nor anyone) Tj
T* (else mentioned Ordinance 691 or indicated that attaching) Tj
T* (lechis to utility poles might be unlawful.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The Council decided to demand a formal, written) Tj
T* (proposal before voting on whether to issue the) Tj
T* (proclamation. Mayor Moscovitz advised Gotlieb and Osen,) Tj
T* (who did not attend the meeting, that the Council was) Tj
T* (unlikely to grant their request for a proclamation, but) Tj
T* (invited them to submit a formal application. Frustrated by) Tj
T* (the Borough's reticence, in August 1999 TEAI asked Bergen) Tj
T* (County Executive William P. Schuber, whose jurisdiction) Tj
T* (includes Tenafly, to issue the ceremonial proclamation) Tj
T* (necessary to validate the eruv. On December 15, 1999, he) Tj
T* (did so. The constitutionality of this action is not challenged) Tj
T* (in this case, and neither Schuber nor any other Bergen) Tj
T* (County official is a party.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Verizon, the local telephone company, owns the utility) Tj
T* (poles in Tenafly, though the poles are located on the) Tj
