T* (Black Horse Pike, 84 F.3d at 1484, even after Zelman, Good News Club,) Tj
T* (and Santa Fe eschewed it in favor of the endorsement test, it does not) Tj
T* (support the Borough's Establishment Clause defense. Allowing the eruv) Tj
T* (to remain in place serves the secular purpose of complying with the Free) Tj
T* (Exercise Clause, does not have the effect of advancing religion because) Tj
T* (no reasonable, informed observer would perceive an endorsement of) Tj
T* (religion, and involves no government entanglement with religion because) Tj
T* (the Borough will not monitor or support the maintenance of the eruv.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                45) Tj
T* () Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Our review of the record leaves us convinced that, in) Tj
T* (addition to the reasonable probability that the plaintiffs will) Tj
T* (ultimately prevail on their free exercise claim, the) Tj
T* (remaining three factors for injunctive relief--irreparable) Tj
T* (injury, the balance of hardships, and the public interest--) Tj
T* (also favor a preliminary injunction. Limitations on the free) Tj
T* (exercise of religion inflict irreparable injury. Fifth Ave.) Tj
T* (Presbyterian Church v. City of New York, 293 F.3d 570, 574) Tj
T* (\(2d Cir. 2002\); Kikumura v. Hurley, 242 F.3d 950, 963) Tj
T* (\(10th Cir. 2001\); see also Swartzwelder v. McNeilly, 297) Tj
T* (F.3d 228, 241 \(3d Cir. 2002\) \("`The loss of First Amendment) Tj
T* (freedoms, for even minimal periods of time, unquestionably) Tj
T* (constitutes irreparable injury."'\) \(quoting Elrod v. Burns,) Tj
T* (427 U.S. 347, 373 \(1976\)\). The plaintiffs have demonstrated) Tj
T* (that, if the eruv is removed, they will be unable to push and) Tj
T* (carry objects outside the home on the Sabbath, and those) Tj
T* (who are disabled or have small children consequently will) Tj
T* (be unable to attend synagogue. This showing easily) Tj
T* (satisfies the irreparable injury requirement.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (With respect to the balance of hardships, a preliminary) Tj
T* (injunction would not harm the Borough more than denying) Tj
T* (relief would harm the plaintiffs. Enjoining removal of the) Tj
T* (eruv would cause neither the Borough nor its residents any) Tj
T* (serious injury. Without an injunction, on the other hand,) Tj
T* (the plaintiffs' free exercise of religion will be impaired. The) Tj
T* (balance easily tips in the plaintiffs' favor.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Finally, where there are no societal benefits justifying a) Tj
T* (burden on religious freedom, "the public interest clearly) Tj
T* (favors the protection of constitutional rights." Council of) Tj
T* (Alternative Political Parties v. Hooks, 121 F.3d 876, 884 \(3d) Tj
T* (Cir. 1997\). We do not see how removing the lechis could) Tj
T* (advance any interests sufficient to outweigh the) Tj
T* (infringement of the plaintiffs' free exercise rights.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (In this context, the District Court should have) Tj
T* (preliminarily enjoined the Borough from removing the lechis) Tj
T* (pending a trial.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (IV. Conclusion) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Though the plaintiffs are not likely to prevail on their Fair) Tj
T* (Housing Act claim and do not present a viable free speech) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (                                46) Tj
